 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>Reactions to Shays’ Rebellion</p>\n<p>“What, gracious God, is man! That there should be such inconsistency &amp; perfidiousness in his conduct? It is but the other day we were shedding our blood to obtain the Constitutions under which we now live—Constitutions of our own choice and framing—and now we are unsheathing the Sword to overturn them! The thing is so unaccountable, that I hardly know how to realize it, or to persuade myself that I am not under the vision of a dream.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">George Washington to David Humphreys, December 26, 1786</p>\n<p>“No Morn ever dawned more favourable than ours did—and no day was ever more clouded than the present! Wisdom, &amp; good examples are necessary at this time to rescue the political machine from the impending storm. . . . Without some alteration in our political creed, the superstructure we have been seven years raising at the expence of much blood and treasure, must fall. We are fast verging to anarchy &amp; confusion!”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">George Washington to James Madison, November 5, 1786</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpts provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 614982,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following best describes the influence of Shays’ Rebellion on the mindset of many U.S. political leaders such as George Washington?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1167901,
     "content_html": "It demonstrated to them the need for reform in the structure of government under the Articles of Confederation."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167900,
     "content_html": "It led many to change their stance on the U.S. Constitution from federalism to anti-federalism."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167899,
     "content_html": "It caused some to favor a strong executive over a strong legislature."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167898,
     "content_html": "It demonstrated that citizens were not prepared to vote for their leaders."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 614983,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following best explains why Shays’ Rebellion garnered national attention?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1167904,
     "content_html": "It involved a large portion of the population on both sides of the conflict."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167905,
     "content_html": "It was the first significant instance of resistance to the new American republic."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167903,
     "content_html": "It was the first test of the new Constitution, which led many to question its strength."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167902,
     "content_html": "It portrayed the tyranny and domination of the federal government under the Articles of Confederation."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 614984,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What did Washington mean by “the superstructure we have been seven years raising at the expence of much blood and treasure”?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1167908,
     "content_html": "He was angry about the political machine that had taken over and corrupted the government of the United States just seven years after the Revolutionary War ended."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167909,
     "content_html": "The endangered superstructure to which Washington referred in this passage was the new republic under the Articles of Confederation."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167907,
     "content_html": "Washington warned that the only safe political creed existing by 1786 was the form of government existing under the Articles of Confederation,"
    },
    {
     "id": 1167906,
     "content_html": "Washington was pleased and hopeful about the “favorable morn” dawning in 1786, because the authors of the Articles of Confederation had set such a good example of wisdom and leadership."
    }
   ],
